  "problem": "In a rectangular (or Cartesian) system of coordinates, we can think of a point as the vertex of a rectangular solid. For instance, the point $P(1,\\sqrt3,3)$ can be represented as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/47873274f1cc630a8275b3ef2f83210b3d16a4f3d0acabeae9dbda32844a9adf.png)\n\n \n\nWhen we consider the corresponding cylindrical coordinates of $P,$ given by $\\left(2, \\dfrac{\\pi}3, 3\\right)$ in this case, we can think of $P$ as a point that lies on the edge of a cylinder, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14220175/assets/9fefa7c5fac5cac803fdebeaa9eab4233d723b452d2ee8dc17151d992064223f.png)",
